  "worked_solution": "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-8)\\cdot 4+14\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=-2[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-8)\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\text{\u00a0}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
